# Every text file is stored with LF and checked out with LF, whatever the platform or the
# local core.autocrlf. A file an editor saved with CRLF is normalized when it is staged, so
# a commit never carries a whole file nobody changed. Git decides what is text; binaries
# are left alone. The rules below override this one where a file needs something else.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Sources, build files and configuration: text always, never left to detection.
*.c       text eol=lf
*.h       text eol=lf
*.cpp     text eol=lf
*.hpp     text eol=lf
*.inl     text eol=lf
*.cmake   text eol=lf
*.in      text eol=lf
*.init    text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.siml    text eol=lf
*.dtml    text eol=lf
*.fsml    text eol=lf
*.txt     text eol=lf
CMakeLists.txt text eol=lf

# The document schemas and the rule registry are mirrored between this repository and its two
# siblings, and every copy has to be byte-comparable from any checkout. Without this a Windows
# clone stores them with carriage returns, the comparison goes red, and the person who sees it
# reads a line-ending artifact as the mirror having drifted.
*.xsd   text eol=lf
*.xml   text eol=lf

# Scripts are executed, not compared. A shell script stored with carriage returns fails
# on every POSIX system with an error that names the interpreter and not the line ending,
# and a Python tool run from a shebang fails the same way. The agent documentation tells
# an agent to run these by name, so a broken checkout breaks the first instruction.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.bat   text eol=crlf

# PowerShell and Markdown are edited on Windows as often as anywhere else, and an
# editor there writes carriage returns. Undeclared, the checkout then differs from
# the blob and the next commit carries a whole file nobody changed.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.md    text eol=lf
